# Idea Catcher API: the recipe calls, nothing else (VS Code extension "REST Client", id humao.rest-client).
# The same calls as the curl recipe in docs/idea-catcher-first-local-test.md ("Recipe: test the local ic data").
# For all the endpoints, with their error cases, use tests/manual/catcher-api.http.
#
# Before you send anything:
#   1. uv run catcher testdata reset
#   2. docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.local-test.yaml up --build     (wait until the worker is healthy)
# When you are done:
#   3. docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.local-test.yaml down -v          (also deletes the scratch database)
#
# Send the requests in order. The two keys belong to compose.local-test.yaml (public, only for that test).

@baseUrl = http://127.0.0.1:8000
@key = local-test-key-not-a-secret-0001

### 1. Is the stack ready? 200 with {"api":"ok","database":"ok","worker":"running"}
GET {{baseUrl}}/health

### 2a. OPTIONAL: what would a run do? A read-only preview job; it processes nothing (202, then 2b)
# What happens when you send this request:
#   1. The API receives it. The API does no work itself: it only writes ONE row in the Postgres table "jobs"
#      (type pipeline.preview, status "queued") and answers 202 with the job_id.
#   2. The worker (a separate container) finds that queued row and picks it up. It sets the status to "running",
#      reads the files in tmp/ic to work out what a real run would do, and then updates the SAME row to
#      "succeeded", with the report in its "result" column.
#   3. Request 2b reads that row back from Postgres.
# Nothing else is written: no job_items, no job_events, the YouTube gate is untouched and no file in tmp/ic
# changes. The row stays until the database is deleted (down -v). Sending 2a again makes another row.
# @name preview
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"dry_run": true}

### 2b. The preview job: status "succeeded" after a few seconds, then result.report.counts
# This call only READS from Postgres and writes nothing. The API looks up the one row of the preview job
# in the table "jobs" (by the job_id from 2a) and returns its properties: status, parameters, timings,
# an error if there was one, and "result", the report the worker saved there.
# The documents are in result.report.names.items: one entry per document in the inbox, with its name,
# doc_id, doc_class, a status (for example would_publish) and llm_saved. They come from the saved report,
# not from document records: a preview creates no job_items rows. "queued" or "running" in the status
# means the worker is not done yet: send 2b again.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs/{{preview.response.body.$.job_id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 3. QUEUE THE DATA without the LLM and without YouTube: one pipeline.run job with the fake profile (202)
# This is the same call as 2a (POST /api/v1/pipeline/runs), but WITHOUT "dry_run": so it is a real run that
# PROCESSES the data. The API again only writes one "queued" row in the table "jobs" (type pipeline.run) and
# answers 202. The worker then picks it up and does the work: it stages every document of the inbox, creates
# the document records (job_items) and one llm.reason job per document, and it writes the pages in
# tmp/ic/epiaku-docs. The result is saved in the job row; read it with request 5.
# Still no LLM and no YouTube: "profile": "fake" makes the LLM step use the fake backend (no model is
# called), YouTube is switched off in this stack and the test data holds the saved YouTube facts.
# @name startRun
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"profile": "fake"}

### 3b. Send the same request again right away: 200, "existing": true, the SAME job_id (no second run is queued)
# Purpose: to see that the API does not queue a second run while one is still waiting or running.
# What it means: you start a run that is already queued or running. The API finds that run and answers
#   200 (not 202) with "existing": true and the job_id of the FIRST run (the same id as in request 3).
#   Nothing new is written in Postgres: no second row in the table "jobs". Any options in the body are ignored.
# Why: two starts should not process the same inbox twice. It protects against a double click, two people or
#   devices starting a run, and the API starting a run while a scheduled run is already waiting.
# Timing: the run takes only a few seconds on the test data, so send 3b right after 3. If the run has already
#   finished, nothing is waiting any more: 3b then starts a NEW run (202, a new job_id). That run finds an
#   empty inbox and processes nothing.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"profile": "fake"}

### 4a. HOW MANY JOBS? Read "total" in each answer. Jobs waiting in the queue:
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=queued&limit=1
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 4b. Jobs running now:
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=running&limit=1
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 4c. Jobs finished: about 44 on the test data (the run + one llm.reason job per document, 43)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=succeeded&limit=1
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 4d. Jobs failed: 0
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=failed&limit=1
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 5. This run: status, result with the counts, item_counts per class and status
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs/{{startRun.response.body.$.job_id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 6a. The documents: "total" is 43 on the test data
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/items?limit=1
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 6b. The documents that are published: "total" is 43 when the run has finished
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/items?status=published&limit=1
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
